# Sweep the sensor plate across one wall partition with the ARM, base parked.
#
# ARM_SWEEP_PLAN §7.B. The base-driven sweep it replaces could not hold the plate
# on the wall: force mode's compliant axis cannot keep up with even the slowest
# Nav2 velocity, and the global planner arcs the path away from walls. Here the
# base does not move at all -- `arm_base` is static for the whole goal, which is
# what makes solving the whole sweep in that frame safe.

# --- Goal ---
# Partition endpoints. The authoritative sweep direction is normalize(end - start);
# the plate-derived direction is only ever a sanity check (§4.2).
geometry_msgs/Point start
geometry_msgs/Point end
# Which partition this is, for the diagnostics identity only -- the executor's
# behaviour does not depend on it.
int32 partition_index
int32 partition_count
# Frame the endpoints are expressed in. Transformed into the arm's planning frame
# once, at goal acceptance.
string frame_id
# Commanded Cartesian speed along the wall, m/s. <= 0 uses the node's
# sweep_speed_mps parameter. Waypoint times are derived as t_i = s_i / speed, so
# this is the single control over how fast the plate crosses the wall.
float64 speed
# True when Force Mode is pressing the plate against the wall, so the contact
# watchdog knows whether losing contact is a fault or the expected standoff case
# (Gazebo has no force mode: the sweep runs contact-free at the plate offset).
bool press
# Positioning-only goal: run the LEAD-IN and stop, without sweeping.
#
# The FSM's arm approach can only move the plate along the wall normal (the
# partition centre sits on the base centreline, where the arm planner's column
# obstacle is), so the lateral move to the partition start belongs to this node.
# Doing it as its own goal is what lets the FSM press the plate against the wall
# at the partition START rather than at its centre: force mode is started between
# this goal and the sweep goal, so the GPR wheel is never dragged sideways across
# the wall under load. The sweep goal that follows starts from wherever the press
# left the plate -- it never traverses under load, even if the press drifted the
# plate a few centimetres from the nominal partition start.
bool lead_in_only
---
# --- Result ---
bool success
# Machine-readable failure reason, matching SweepPlanError.reason where the
# failure came from trajectory generation: ik_unreachable, branch_discontinuity,
# joint_velocity, near_singular, interpolation_error, seed_not_at_start, ...
# plus the execution-side ones: contact_lost, controller_failed, cancelled.
string reason
string detail
# How far the plate actually travelled along the partition tangent, and how long
# it took -- the two numbers that say whether the sweep ran at the speed asked for.
float64 swept_length
float64 duration
---
# --- Feedback ---
# "lead_in" while the arm travels from where it was parked (the partition CENTRE,
# where the base stops) to the partition start; "sweep" once it is actually
# crossing the wall. The FSM starts the GPR line on that transition -- starting it
# at goal acceptance would record the lead-in travel as scan data.
string phase
float64 progress             # 0..1 along the partition
float64 lateral_speed        # measured wall-tangent TCP speed, m/s
float64 wall_distance        # mean plate-to-wall distance from the six sensors, m
float64 lateral_error        # TCP deviation along the tangent, m
float64 normal_error         # TCP deviation along the wall normal, m
int32 replans                # orientation-correction replans so far (§4.2)
